---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CitationBlock from "../../../components/CitationBlock.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../../components/PageIntro.astro";
import ScholarlyMeta from "../../../components/ScholarlyMeta.astro";
import SectionBlock from "../../../components/SectionBlock.astro";
import UseCaseFit from "../../../components/UseCaseFit.astro";
import {
  createImplementationPublication,
  implementationExampleAnchorLinks,
} from "../../../content/implementation-examples";

const pageTitle =
  "An AI agent acting for one person: implementation comparison";
const pageDescription =
  "An assistant that sends email and pays bills for one person, governed two ways: settings and a confirm button, or a grant that ends, a prompt that can change the outcome, and records the person checks it against.";
const permalink = "/use-cases/examples/personal-agent";
const recordsBase = "/standards/worked-examples/personal-agent";
const anchorLinks = [
  ...implementationExampleAnchorLinks.filter(
    (link) => link.href !== "#fit" && link.href !== "#citations",
  ),
  { href: "#record", label: "The grant as a record" },
  ...implementationExampleAnchorLinks.filter(
    (link) => link.href === "#fit" || link.href === "#citations",
  ),
];
const publication = {
  ...createImplementationPublication(
    permalink,
    "Published the comparison for an agent acting for one person.",
  ),
  published: "2026-10-13T00:00:00Z",
  updated: "2026-10-13T00:00:00Z",
  changelog: [
    {
      version: "v1.0.0",
      date: "2026-10-13",
      summary: "Published the comparison for an agent acting for one person.",
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  publishedTime="2026-10-13T00:00:00.000Z"
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Implementation example"
    title={pageTitle}
    description={pageDescription}
    anchorLinks={anchorLinks}
  />

  <SectionBlock
    id="overview"
    eyebrow="Overview"
    title="Where governance breaks down"
    description="The person who delegates is the person the agent's mistakes land on, and what they hold are settings."
  >
    <p>
      A person connects an assistant to their email, their calendar, one card,
      and their health insurer's portal. It drafts replies, books appointments,
      pays recurring bills, and resubmits a claim the insurer rejected. Reading
      and drafting run without a prompt. Sending and paying wait for a tap.
    </p>
    <p>
      No institution operates this delegation. The person granted it and can end
      it, so the framework does less here than where an institution decides
      about many people. What remains is the delegation itself, and the people
      the agent writes to and pays, who never agreed to it.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="standard"
    eyebrow="Standard governance"
    title="What an assistant usually offers"
    description="Permissions granted once, a confirm button, and a history the vendor keeps."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <ul class="card__list">
      <li>
        Permissions granted at setup that last until the person revokes them.
      </li>
      <li>A confirm button before a message is sent or a bill is paid.</li>
      <li>An activity history kept in the vendor's app.</li>
      <li>A memory of the person that they can view and delete.</li>
      <li>Terms of service that set what the vendor answers for.</li>
    </ul>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="ethotechnics"
    eyebrow="Ethotechnics implementation"
    title="What changes, and what to check before adding a power"
    description="The person holds a grant they can read, renew, and end, and checks the agent against records it does not write."
  >
    <div class="stack-sm">
      <ul class="card__list">
        <li>
          The grant names what the agent may send and pay, and when the grant
          ends: a date, not "until revoked." At renewal, three quiet months are
          not a reason on their own. The person names what they would have seen
          if the agent were failing, as
          <a href="/standards/std-08-delegation">STD-08 §1.2</a> requires of any renewal.
        </li>
        <li>
          Adding a power, from sending to paying, is a decision with its own
          date in the record. Scope that grew by habit is scope nobody granted.
        </li>
        <li>
          Before an action, the agent says whether it can be undone in practice.
          A sent email can be recalled for 30 seconds and not after it has been
          read. A card payment can be disputed, on the bank's clock.
        </li>
        <li>
          The confirm button is a control only if the person can change the
          outcome from it: edit the message, change the amount, refuse. If the
          person approves every prompt for a month, the prompt is what gets
          reviewed, not the person.
        </li>
        <li>
          The person checks the agent against records it does not write: the
          sent folder and the card statement. The activity history is the
          agent's account of itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          A correction changes the agent's standing instruction, and the agent
          says what changed. Fixing one reply and leaving the instruction in
          place means making the same correction next week. The eighth of the
          <a href="/standards/laws#law-viii">twelve laws</a> puts it as "observability
          without state transition is theater."
        </li>
        <li>
          When the agent fixes another institution's error, a rejected claim or
          a double charge, it records the error as well as the fix. Otherwise
          the insurer's error rate looks lower than it is, and the person's time
          stops being counted.
          <a href="/research/theory/absorption-as-concealment"
            >Absorption as concealment</a
          > describes the pattern.
        </li>
        <li>
          The people the agent writes to and pays have no channel to it. A reply
          reaches the person, but nothing tells the recipient an assistant
          wrote.
        </li>
        <li>
          The person keeps the skills the agent replaced. A week without it,
          before adding a power, shows whether they still can.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted">
        Before adding a power, the person checks what the agent does without
        asking, that a stop works partway through a task, and how someone the
        agent wrote to can reach them. They also count the prompts they edited
        or refused last month, and the errors the agent fixed in other
        institutions' systems, by institution.
      </p>
    </div>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="record"
    eyebrow="The record"
    title="The grant, written down"
    description="This delegation as an STD-08 authority grant, filled in with what a person can show."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <p>
      The <a href={`${recordsBase}/authority-grant.json`}>authority grant</a> and
      the confirm prompt's <a href={`${recordsBase}/intervention-spec.json`}
        >intervention specification</a
      > validate against the published schemas. Three fields assume an institution,
      and the records say so instead of filling them:
    </p>
    <ul class="muted">
      <li>
        The attestation asks for a signature. Nothing signs a permission set in
        an app.
      </li>
      <li>
        Liability names the party that accepted it. Until the person reads the
        vendor's terms for that, the record names the person.
      </li>
      <li>
        The prompt's specification asks for protection against retaliation. No
        one can penalize the person for refusing, so it does not apply.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The specification also records the prompt's weakness: approving takes one
      tap, and nothing rewards reading it.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the agent asks to take this framework on,
      <a href="/agents">what it should ask, and write down for the person</a>.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <UseCaseFit slug="personal-agent" />

  <div id="citations">
    <CitationBlock
      title={pageTitle}
      permalink={permalink}
      publication={publication}
      summaryLabel="Cite this implementation example"
    >
      <ScholarlyMeta bare publication={publication} permalink={permalink} />
    </CitationBlock>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
